during this process.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:35.080
So some history.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.990
So when I joined Spotify, it
was three years and 1/2 ago.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:44.300
And we just started
moving to GCP, I remember.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.250
And I'd been exposed marginally
to what was there before.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.710
There was basically
on-prem data centers.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:51.920
We had four of them--

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.930
two in the US and two in Europe.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.990
And so we used to
manage our machines.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:03.100
And being in the storage
infrastructure team

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.020
at the time, and I still am,
we had basically two databases.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:09.340
So it looked like this.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.040
We had Cassandra and Postgres.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.460
So that means that we also
managed our databases, not just

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.410
the machines.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:22.930
So in this talk, I'm going to
focus quite a lot on Cassandra,

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:26.200
because it became extremely
popular at Spotify,

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.250
and I'm going to start telling
you why it became so popular.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.910
In a word, Cassandra
is versatile,

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:37.470
so it can do many
things quite well.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.480
It has tunable consistency.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:40.803
It has tunable performance.

00:01:40.803 --> 00:01:42.220
It has a lot of
knobs that you can

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:48.715
tune to almost optimize at the
use case level, I would say.

00:01:48.715 --> 00:01:51.240
It has global application.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.760
It scales pretty well from very
small, like order of megabytes

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:00.090
to terabytes in terms
of data set size.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.430
And it comes with a handy
query language similar to SQL,

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.530
and lightweight transactions.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:10.120
So you can express things
like "INSERT if not exist,"

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:14.190
which is a pretty
powerful feature.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.750
These features made Cassandra
the database for Spotify,

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:17.260
as I said.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.962
And here are some
numbers that I digged--

00:02:20.962 --> 00:02:22.420
took me quite a
while to find that,

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.820
because from when
Cassandra was at peak,

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.360
that was approximately
one year and 1/2 ago.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:38.640
So yeah, a lot of machines'
load clusters, and in that--

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.880
but Cassandra-- yeah.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.370
Even if it was widely
used, it's not perfect.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.070
It comes with a set of problems.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.500
I would say the biggest
is the fact that storage

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.600
is coupled to compute, and
this makes topology changes

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.930
very expensive.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:00.060
Because think like, for example,
you are running out of disk,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.170
and you are forced to
scale out your cluster,

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.440
even if maybe you don't need
more compute, for example.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.860
And topology changes needs
to shuffle things around,

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.170
so it takes a lot
of time, especially

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.600
if you have a lot of data.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.760
As I said, it's very
tunable, which is good.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.690
But it's also bad sometimes,
because it's too much.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:25.040
It has a very long
learning curve.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.340
It means like teams needs to be
experts at operating Cassandra.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.480
And in our case, we managed some
of these clusters-- the most

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:34.230
critical ones.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:40.550
But in general we believe in
the so-called opposing squads,

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:45.720
so that the teams manage
their own clusters.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.660
So yeah, it comes with a
pretty high operational burden

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.320
in that sense.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:56.170
It scales until it
doesn't, pretty much.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:59.250
We had some cases
where we really

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.140
felt like we
couldn't add any more

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.650
nodes to a specific cluster
or it would blow up.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:09.720
And the Cassandra version we
used, which is not DataStax,

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.810
doesn't come with data
exports and backups.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:15.900
So also that was tricky.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.670
In my team, we used to
build tools, basically,

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:24.220
to alleviate these pains.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.800
So we provide automation to
other teams in the company.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.200
And all sorts of things
that I already mentioned,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.250
like for example, we have
tools for just configuration,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:40.870
and updating and
topology changes.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:44.070
One of them is cstar that we
open sourced, that is basically

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.960
a parallel SSH, topology aware.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.475
So it knows-- for example, if
you want to restart Cassandra,

00:04:50.475 --> 00:04:52.680
it knows in which
order you should

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:56.670
do it to avoid having
unavailable data,

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.470
and unavailable ranges of data.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:00.810
We have cassandra-reaper.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.060
That maybe is the most
famous tool we wrote.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:06.300
It's about [INAUDIBLE] repairs.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.230
We wrote tools for data
exports, like Hezo.

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:12.090
And Hecuba is for
topology changes--

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.570
all sorts of things.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.480
And we eventually got
to a pretty good spot.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:21.030
Like say, pretty much,
give me a Cassandra cluster

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:24.960
with this number of nodes,
with this machine type,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.760
with backups and exports
on a schedule, and yeah.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.290
So pretty much quite
easy, but there's

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.660
a lot of work in
maintaining all these tools,

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.480
and some operations are
still extremely expensive.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:39.780
Like imagine you want
to update the operating

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.400
system on 3,000, 4,000 hosts.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.640
It takes a long time.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:51.550
In this setting, we started
looking at cloud providers,

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.920
and we eventually chose GCP.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.760
Not only because of
storage, but the [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.520
was framed in a much
bigger scope, of course.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.160
And the idea was to basically
move up the stack, in a sense.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.860
So free as much as
possible our time,

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.470
and do things more closely
related to our business logic,

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:16.940
instead of doing infrastructure
and managing machines.

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:18.340
It should be cheaper, also.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:21.300
And yeah, that was
basically the idea.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:27.720
And it should also
be possible to--

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.140
so having more time and working
at different abstraction

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:33.730
level to add value on top
of these technologies.

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:35.470
And we have many
success stories of this,

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:40.780
like Scio for
scheduling jobs in--

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.750
all sorts of jobs, I guess.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:43.250
No, sorry.

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:46.570
Styx is for scheduling, and
Scio is for [INAUDIBLE] jobs.

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:49.870
Forseti-- security.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:53.280
And the autoscaler, I'm
going to talk about later.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.220
I would say we mostly succeeded
in achieving these things,

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.070
and now we are fully on GCP.

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:02.290
But even if you're fully
on GCP, the situation

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:05.830
look like this for a while more.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:10.090
So basically, we just migrated
the physical machines to VMs,

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:11.890
and worked fairly well.

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.870
But at some point,
we ask ourselves,

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:16.270
can we use something
that Google is providing?

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:19.360
Like managed storage,
like Cloud Bigtable?

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:21.410
And I will give a--

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:25.046
Sandhya's going to continue
talking about that.

00:07:25.046 --> 00:07:26.950
SANDHYA GHAI: Thanks, Emilio.

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:29.350
Yeah, I just want to provide
a brief overview of Cloud

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:31.630
Bigtable and where
we think it delivers

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:35.190
customers value, like Spotify.

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.280
So really quickly,
what is Cloud Bigtable?

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.350
Cloud Bigtable is
a petabyte-scale,

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.230
fully managed NoSQL database.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:46.800
The original Bigtable technology
was built to index the web,

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.520
and in its history, has served
billion-user applications

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.990
within Google, like
Gmail and Search.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:56.640
The Cloud product is meant
to serve use cases where

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.270
low latency data access,
scalability, and reliability

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:01.440
are critical.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:05.190
As a fully-managed service,
it scales seamlessly,

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:08.190
and we're also well-integrated
with the Apache ecosystem,

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:09.885
offering an HBase API.

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:17.330
And Bigtable sits within
a broader portfolio

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:19.970
of database and storage
products, all of which

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:22.400
remove the burden of
building and managing

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.290
storage and infrastructure.

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:27.700
Specifically, it sits in
the non-relational space

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:29.330
as a wide column store.

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:31.430
Again, offering super
high performance

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:35.620
in terms of latency
and throughput.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:37.679
So what can Cloud
Bigtable offer you?

00:08:37.679 --> 00:08:39.510
Where does the value come from?

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.710
I tend to focus on four areas--

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:47.070
speed, scalability, full
management, and integrations.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:50.250
I'll talk about speed and
scalability more in a second,

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.800
because I think that's what's
differentiating about Bigtable.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.310
And integration with
a broader ecosystem

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.770
is what allows it to plug
easily into your existing

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:00.050
architecture.

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:02.255
But I want to pause for a
second on full management,

00:09:02.255 --> 00:09:04.380
because this is something
we're thinking about very

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:05.830
holistically.

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:09.870
What does it-- what can we do
to lessen the operational cost

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:11.677
of managing a database?

00:09:11.677 --> 00:09:14.010
And so some of that, yes, is
managing the infrastructure

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:19.470
and database layer, having RSREs
paged so that you're not paged.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.980
It also means delivering the
availability, data durability,

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:26.280
and security that you've
come to expect from Google.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:27.810
But for me, it also
means providing

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:30.360
value add tools to make
the database easier

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.400
to work with in every way.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.850
So one quick example of
that is Key Visualizer,

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:38.460
which we launched last year
at Next, which is basically

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:43.980
a heat map of your key space
and shows data access patterns.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.230
So again, not only are we
managing that infrastructure

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.140
and keeping you
from getting paged,

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:52.020
we're also giving you
visibility into your data

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.940
and making the
parts of the process

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:57.370
that you own, like schema
design, a lot easier.

00:09:57.370 --> 00:09:59.220
So again, as a
managed service, we're

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:04.130
thinking a lot about how to
reduce that operational burden.

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:06.920
And as promised, jumping
into speed again.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:09.320
This is where I think
Bigtable is unique.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:11.780
On the throughput
side, we're offering

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:14.480
10 megabytes per second
in write throughput,

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:19.100
and 220 megabits per second
in read scan throughput.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:23.240
I want to emphasize here that
generally in Bigtable, reads

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.320
and writes are the same price.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.320
So customers with write-heavy
workloads, in particular,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:31.070
will see excised
performance because

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.500
of that high write throughput.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:36.920
And we also offer low
latency random data access

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:38.780
in the single-digit
milliseconds.

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:44.080
Scalability-- so
I think there are

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:46.570
two things to focus on here.

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:48.490
Again, Bigtable
was built for scale

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:52.630
and has the ability to handle
internet-sized applications.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:54.730
That's because performance,
which we measure in

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:58.150
QPS, Queries Per
Second, scales linearly

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:00.220
as you increase the
number of nodes.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:01.720
I think our published
guidelines say

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:05.930
that you should expect
10,000 QPS per node,

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:10.620
and that's true whether you
have three nodes or 300.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:14.900
And second, you can scale up and
scale down with Bigtable super

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:16.083
easily.

00:11:16.083 --> 00:11:17.750
Again, this is what
makes Bigtable price

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:20.990
effective for spiky workloads,
because you can scale up

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:25.000
to handle the spike and back
down when things cool down.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:27.950
And Emilio's going to speak
a little bit about how

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.680
they leveraged this
characteristic in building

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.555
their autoscaler to
get cost savings.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:38.210
To dive a little bit
deeper on how Bigtable

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:42.710
is able to deliver that
performance and scale,

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:45.710
I want to touch a little
bit on how Bigtable works.

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:48.110
So Bigtable separates
the processing layer

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:50.360
from the storage
layer, which allows

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:54.080
us to optimize throughput
by adjusting the association

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:56.940
of storage and nodes.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.840
So in this example, we
start with three nodes

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:02.960
and four splits of the data
that are stored separately

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:04.540
in our storage layer.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:06.740
And in the rebalancing
example, we

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:10.490
might be seeing heavy
traffic to data group A.

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:14.930
And so node one is experiencing
a large load in this case.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:16.460
Bigtable is going
to automatically

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:20.450
rebalance and move some of
that traffic to another load,

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:24.950
ensuring broader, better
overall performance.

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:26.750
And again, resizing
comes into play

00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:29.130
when another node
is added to the mix.

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:30.620
And again, we're
able to rebalance

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:35.120
without any downtime, because
instead of moving data around,

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:38.770
we're just reassigning the
node to a different data group.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:45.400
I also wanted to touch
base on one feature

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.880
that's been recently
made available,

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:51.130
and that Spotify has leveraged
pretty intensely, which

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:54.700
is Cloud Bigtable Replication.

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:59.110
Replication allows you to
increase your availability

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:05.560
with a 99.99% SLA by
allowing you to failover

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:07.510
in the case of a geo disaster.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:11.560
It also allows you to isolate
batch and serving workloads.

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:13.760
And finally, if you're
replicating globally,

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:17.530
like Spotify does, you can
serve a global user base

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:20.470
by offering customers
low latency data

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:22.690
access to local replicas.

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:28.530
There are a few things that
I think are special here.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:30.900
You write data once,
and you can write data

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:32.850
to any one of those replicas.

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:34.740
And it's automatically
replicated everywhere

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.680
with zero consistency.

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:39.510
And finally, there
are no manual steps,

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:42.360
so you're not writing
tools to repair data

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.970
or synchronize
writes and deletes.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:49.902
Replication just works, and
will eventually be consistent.

00:13:52.958 --> 00:13:55.000
We have a-- if you're
interested in learning more

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.520
about replication,
I hope you'll join

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.620
Carter Page, our
engineering manager,

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:01.900
in talking about it tomorrow.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:07.210
That is DBS 307 at 2:10
PM, Building a Global Data

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:07.780
Presence.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:09.432
That's my quick plug.

00:14:09.432 --> 00:14:13.180
But yeah, handing it
back over to Emilio

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:15.820
to talk about how they
approach the migration.

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:17.960
EMILIO DEL
TESSANDORO: Thank you.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:20.650
So yeah, our migration--

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:25.230
well, we have first just a short
recap about what Sandhya said.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:27.930
The Cloud Bigtable is
managed, is eventually

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:32.700
consistent like Cassandra, but
no lightweight transactions.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:34.470
Storage is decoupled
from compute

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:37.090
and is designed
for big data sets.

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:39.810
So the first thing
you might ask yourself

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:43.676
is, is Cassandra the
same thing as Bigtable?

00:14:43.676 --> 00:14:45.990
And yeah, the answer
is, most of the time,

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:51.360
you can map data sets that you
had on Cassandra to Bigtable.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:55.170
We found at Spotify that
the most common scenarios

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:57.540
that we can map very
well are eventually

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:01.620
consistent or low
consistency needs when

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:06.060
we have ephemeral or
semi-persistent data.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.970
Maybe you have regionally
partitioned data.

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:13.860
And by this, I mean that
a certain set of the data

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:15.480
is accessed only
from certain regions.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:18.300
Like for example, European
data is only accessed in Europe

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:22.200
and so on, because they
have some cool features that

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:23.880
are available in that way--

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:27.600
the single row
transactions, basically.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:30.130
And of course, large data sets.

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:33.540
This use case map very well.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:35.490
Other use cases,
they don't map well.

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.620
We found that we had,
as you can imagine,

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:44.930
some cases where we use
this "INSERT if not exist."

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:47.070
And this is not
available in Bigtable.

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:52.900
So you simply have to
find something else.

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.590
And also, we have
quite a lot, and this

00:15:55.590 --> 00:16:00.090
was unexpected at the beginning,
of this so-called small key

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:02.250
value stores, we call.

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:04.230
So we have not much data--

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:08.250
maybe order of from
megabytes to a few gigabytes.

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:11.600
But still, you need to
have a global replication,

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:15.390
so to have low latency globally.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:18.600
And then you have very, very
low, or sometimes very high,

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:19.740
QPS.

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:23.290
This also doesn't map well.

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:27.870
So summarizing,
this is the truth

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:30.060
that we found during
our migration.

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:33.630
So there is a pretty big overlap
between Cassandra and Bigtable

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:38.190
for this low consistency and
high throughput scenarios.

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:42.510
And we have these two use
cases that they call "small k-v

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:45.180
stores" and more consistent.

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:49.680
And I am fairly sure
that there is nothing

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:52.060
in the blue part of this graph.

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:54.540
So it's all one of these
three cases, basically.

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:57.390
At least at Spotify.

00:16:57.390 --> 00:17:00.750
It took a while
to find out this.

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:03.150
I suggest if you're doing
a migration like this,

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:05.880
try to figure out these
things beforehand.

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:06.960
Just don't say, oh, yeah.

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:08.718
Go and use Bigtable,
and-- because people

00:17:08.718 --> 00:17:10.260
is going to come
back to you and say,

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:12.052
well yeah, but we don't
have these features

00:17:12.052 --> 00:17:14.020
and now it doesn't work.

00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:16.817
And for us, was
also kind of funny,

00:17:16.817 --> 00:17:18.900
because at the beginning,
we had two features that

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:21.720
were very important for
us that were missing--

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:25.260
backups, for which we
built an internal stopgap

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:27.390
solution, and
global replication,

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:29.030
that now is available.

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:35.280
But at the time, we
built a custom replicator

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:37.570
based on Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:41.160
And really, it was a potentially
consistent replicator,

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:44.550
because at some point, we
found that basically, you could

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:46.627
lose some data, especially--

00:17:46.627 --> 00:17:48.210
if I remember
correctly, was something

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:51.900
about ordering [INAUDIBLE]
inserts and deletes.

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:54.580
Yeah, so it was pretty funny
that after two weeks, oh, yeah.

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:55.080
Oh, wow.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:55.872
We could lose data.

00:17:55.872 --> 00:17:57.840
Yeah.

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:01.410
Anyway, these two features
now are available and yeah,

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:03.310
we use them a lot.

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:06.843
At least-- sorry, the
global replication backups.

00:18:06.843 --> 00:18:08.676
Maybe you're going to
get a question about--

00:18:08.676 --> 00:18:10.620
[LAUGHS]

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:16.880
And so now immigration
is going slowly.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.540
We are, I would say, halfway.

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:22.315
But yeah, we see that the
number of Bigtable nodes

00:18:22.315 --> 00:18:26.405
is increasing, and the number of
Cassandra nodes is decreasing.

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:32.510
As a success story
of migration, I

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:36.488
would like to talk about
the playback cluster.

00:18:36.488 --> 00:18:41.270
That basically implements-- is
very crucial for implementing

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:42.800
the Connect feature.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:44.810
If you're familiar
with that, any store

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:47.300
is basically the device state.

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:50.420
So we have multiple
devices, like TV speakers,

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:53.150
and store the state
of these devices.

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:54.800
The state can be
pretty big, depending

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:58.090
on how the device works.

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:04.490
And it's low consistency data.

00:19:04.490 --> 00:19:07.390
If you lose a state or you
just didn't generate a new one,

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:09.620
it's not a super big deal.

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:12.860
And you need a very,
very high performance.

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:15.830
Because imagine you skip
a song, you go off--

00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:17.920
that's a change
of the state, so--

00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:20.930
and if you count how many users,
how many devices there are,

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:25.010
it's like hundreds of
thousands of requests.

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:29.200
In particular, we used to
have on Cassandra 150,000

00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:31.460
QPS at peak.

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:36.200
In three data centers, we
used to have 450 machines

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:41.660
with 7,000 cohorts, more or
less, and 2.5 petabytes of SSD,

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:44.030
just for this cluster.

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:45.620
And this was one of
the examples where

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:48.680
I was saying, like this cluster
was really pushed to the limit.

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:53.670
We couldn't add another
100 nodes to this cluster.

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:57.110
The team managing this cluster,
we used to have, I believe,

00:19:57.110 --> 00:20:02.190
one incident a day, during
the last weeks of life.

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:07.550
And as a fun fact, or I
would say what was tragically

00:20:07.550 --> 00:20:10.400
bad about this
cluster, is that we

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:13.940
were using just very
little of the disk

00:20:13.940 --> 00:20:16.520
for the actual unique
data, because we

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:19.910
had to over-provision a
lot in terms of disk space

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:22.970
to handle Cassandra compactions.

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:25.970
You don't want to run out
of disk space in a Cassandra

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:27.750
cluster, and we had
to provision a lot

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:29.630
to prevent that, basically.

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:32.040
And the data is very ephemeral.

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:35.480
So yeah, a lot of compactions.

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:38.420
On Bigtable, it
worked out very well.

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:42.230
We recently migrated, like
three, four months ago.

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:43.270
The team is very happy.

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:46.460
They didn't have any
operational headaches so far.

00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:50.380
We autoscaled this cluster,
in between 150-250 nodes,

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:52.400
so we also have less nodes.

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:55.190
And we pay for the
data that we use,

00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:58.130
so we have approximately
40 terabytes of unique data

00:20:58.130 --> 00:21:01.130
and just pay for that.

00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:03.950
It was also nice, because
we've worked pretty closely

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:08.230
with Google engineers that
were developing a new client--

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:09.730
Java client.

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:12.090
And working with the
Emulator is also cool,

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:15.880
so yeah, it was a
very nice experience.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:20.800
And this is a chart of
the cost drop the day

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:23.320
we shut down the
Cassandra machines.

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:29.390
You can see in green
the Compute Engine cost.

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:31.480
It doesn't goes down to
zero, because we also

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:34.540
have the service
nodes in the project.

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:38.830
But nevertheless, it's
approximately a 75% drop

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:40.750
in cost, which is pretty big.

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:47.030
Just a few words on how
we did this migration.

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:50.480
It's an online migration,
so we didn't say,

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:53.300
OK, now the feature is disabled
for a few hours and we swapped,

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:56.260
but it was always online.

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:59.930
What we did is we implemented
a storage interface

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:05.540
based on Bigtable, and we run
that together with Cassandra.

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:08.660
And of course, Cassandra
was the primary database

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:15.860
for this process, and we
slowly sent traffic to Bigtable

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:20.840
in order to detect bugs, to
detect problems, or understand

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:21.470
performance.

00:22:21.470 --> 00:22:24.470
In general, to gain confidence
that everything works.

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:27.387
And by doing that, you
can also compare the data

00:22:27.387 --> 00:22:28.970
that you read from
Cassandra, the data

00:22:28.970 --> 00:22:30.740
that you read from
Bigtable, and see

00:22:30.740 --> 00:22:32.870
that everything makes sense.

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:36.650
And so yeah, as I was
saying, the more confidence

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:39.920
you get, the more traffic
you can send to Bigtable,

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:42.950
and eventually 100%
read and write.

00:22:42.950 --> 00:22:46.940
And when you feel
good, you do the swap

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:48.500
between Cassandra and Bigtable.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:50.343
And eventually,
you drop Cassandra.

00:22:50.343 --> 00:22:51.260
So it's pretty simple.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:58.050
It's pretty simple, because
this kind of data is ephemeral.

00:22:58.050 --> 00:23:02.070
So eventually, basically
your two database

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:03.100
converge the same data.

00:23:03.100 --> 00:23:07.590
And if you have a few errors,
it's fine in this case,

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:09.510
or in cases like this.

00:23:09.510 --> 00:23:12.990
And in cases where you
have persistent data,

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:16.080
so you cannot tolerate errors--

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:20.370
like let's say our
playlists cluster.

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:23.040
It's much more tricky, at least
with the Cassandra version

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:24.710
that we have.

00:23:24.710 --> 00:23:27.830
In general, it's tricky because
keeping in sync to distributed

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:31.620
databases is not the best.

00:23:31.620 --> 00:23:36.870
But there is a pretty
difficult problem

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:38.820
to solve around deletes.

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:44.700
That even if you sync often the
data from the two databases,

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:47.460
like-- the deletes are
not in the data export,

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:50.460
unless you do logical deletes.

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:51.960
If you don't do
logical deletes, you

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:55.530
need to change the schema,
which is also very painful.

00:23:55.530 --> 00:23:57.510
Google has a solution
for this, which

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:02.480
is based on newer versions of a
Cassandra, which we don't use.

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:05.410
Yeah, we are
working around this.

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:08.160
Another thing we
are working on is

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:13.470
trying to obstruct, let's
say, the database layer.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:19.350
So having an interface
that obstructs away

00:24:19.350 --> 00:24:21.660
which database we are using.

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:24.690
Like you may interact with
Cloud Spanner or Cloud Bigtable

00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:27.600
or Cassandra more or
less in the same way.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:29.350
And this is something
more for the future,

00:24:29.350 --> 00:24:31.320
so maybe if we have
to do this again,

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:33.090
it's going to be
much less painful.

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:36.030
But we could also implement
pretty cool things,

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:43.380
like doing this dual storage
transparently for the user.

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:45.540
It's a pretty cool project
and hopefully we're

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:47.400
going to open source
it if it works.

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:52.860
Said that, during
this process, we also

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:57.030
developed an
autoscaler, which it

00:24:57.030 --> 00:25:00.630
wouldn't be impossible
with Cassandra at all.

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:04.210
Because as I said, changing the
number of nodes in a Cassandra

00:25:04.210 --> 00:25:08.564
cluster takes time, especially
a big cluster, it take days.

00:25:08.564 --> 00:25:11.730
And so with Bigtable,
it's very, very nice

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:15.480
to scale up and
down within minutes.

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:17.820
It started as a
hack project and it

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:21.420
was taken from bits and
pieces of other autoscaler--

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:24.180
I think we had five
autoscalers at some point,

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:27.360
so we have to build
one to rule them all,

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:30.750
and so we call in the
amazing Bigtable autoscaler.

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:33.210
And it's very simple set up.

00:25:33.210 --> 00:25:38.610
It's a Java microservice with
some state kept in a Cloud SQL

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.090
database.

00:25:41.090 --> 00:25:42.720
It basically-- the
logics more less

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:47.730
fetch the cluster
status every 30 seconds,

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:51.540
I believe,
interconnection or not.

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:53.670
And it's open source.

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:58.590
Before going more into the
details of the logic, which

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:01.290
is very simple, I
just want to show

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:04.970
what is the effect of
using the autoscaler.

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:09.720
Normally-- so when you have a
constant amount of resources--

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:12.870
in this case, a constant
amount of nodes,

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:19.280
you would say that you have
day and night cycles in CPU.

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:23.378
In Cassandra, for example,
that was the case,

00:26:23.378 --> 00:26:25.170
because you have more
users during the day,

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:28.380
less users during the night.

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:30.790
Using autoscaler,
we see the opposite.

00:26:30.790 --> 00:26:33.420
So we can keep the
CPU utilization

00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:37.350
pretty much constant and
changing the resources

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:38.991
over time.

00:26:38.991 --> 00:26:46.410
And yeah, this graph shows
that it works pretty well.

00:26:46.410 --> 00:26:48.960
And it works also for
replicated clusters,

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:53.440
because we do the scaling
at the cluster level,

00:26:53.440 --> 00:26:55.090
not at the instance level.

00:26:55.090 --> 00:27:01.900
So we can scale independently
different regions, basically.

00:27:01.900 --> 00:27:07.030
And the logic, as I was
saying, is very simple.

00:27:07.030 --> 00:27:11.360
So basically, the user
defines a target load,

00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:13.210
and the autoscaler--

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:16.750
so then given you have
a number of nodes, n,

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:19.540
it just tries to calculate
n1, such that the load

00:27:19.540 --> 00:27:21.290
is what the user defined.

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:26.060
It's just an arithmetic
proportion, basically.

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:30.040
And then we have some checks.

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:32.470
We want to respect
some properties,

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:34.990
so it's very simple
state machine.

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:38.710
For example, we want to respect
the storage per node quota.

00:27:38.710 --> 00:27:42.830
Otherwise, the cluster
stop receiving writes.

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:46.250
We want to have n1, so
the new number of nodes

00:27:46.250 --> 00:27:50.210
should be within the
boundaries that the user sets.

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:54.800
And also, we don't want to
scale too frequently, because--

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:59.870
one is because we have a certain
amount of admin operations,

00:27:59.870 --> 00:28:03.470
so there is a quota
on admin operations.

00:28:03.470 --> 00:28:05.870
But also, we have
some latency spikes

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:08.180
associated to upscales
or downscales, which

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:13.440
is also something we are
working with Google to solve.

00:28:13.440 --> 00:28:15.480
Yeah, but as you see,
it's very simple logic.

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:18.580
And for us, worked really well.

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:23.811
The only real problem we found
is related to data jobs--

00:28:23.811 --> 00:28:25.710
Dataflow jobs, in our case--

00:28:25.710 --> 00:28:31.740
because they are like sudden
spikes of load on the cluster,

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:35.310
and the autoscaler
cannot scale fast enough.

00:28:35.310 --> 00:28:38.940
So basically-- and I think
that the general recommendation

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:41.490
from Google is like, if you
are doing a very heavy Dataflow

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:45.330
job, you should upscale
like 20 minutes before.

00:28:45.330 --> 00:28:47.640
And that's the solution.

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:52.420
Another solution could be
throttling the Dataflow jobs.

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:56.130
Indeed, it sounds pretty
crazy, but it's fairly doable

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:57.750
if you use a framework.

00:28:57.750 --> 00:29:00.870
And in our case, we
used a framework, Scio.

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:02.880
So we could implement in Scio.

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:08.190
Say start slow and slowly
increase the rate in a way

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:11.580
that the autoscaler
can cope with that.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:16.700
Another solution could
be to use a predictive--

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:18.450
transforming in a
predictive process,

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:23.600
or integrate some predictions,
like Facebook Prophet.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:25.860
And this would work if
you have Dataflow jobs

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:30.095
on a certain schedule, not if
you have random during the day.

00:29:30.095 --> 00:29:31.720
Right now, we went
with the second one,

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:33.590
because it was the
recommendation,

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:35.980
and it's more or less what
we were already doing.

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:41.300
So yeah-- and it works so far.

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:45.450
So yeah, wrapping
up, managed is great.

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:47.420
We love it.

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:51.740
Our teams are very happy when
moving away from Cassandra.

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:53.330
We think for us it's
the way forward,

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:59.060
because we want to free as
much as our time as possible.

00:29:59.060 --> 00:30:02.030
The migration is
not an easy process.

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:04.590
You still need to
understand what's--

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:09.750
you can't completely forget
how things works, of course.

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:15.650
And I would say if there
is one key takeaway for us

00:30:15.650 --> 00:30:17.480
in our storage
infrastructure theme,

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:20.240
is that you need to
make the process as

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:22.560
easy as possible for the user.

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:31.450
So like documentation, like
evangelizing good migrations.

00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:33.440
The most you can
do-- or tooling also.

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:36.810
The most you can do for
them, the better it is.

00:30:36.810 --> 00:30:39.980
And yeah, autoscaling is
a great example of value

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:42.920
that we can evangelize.

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:44.480
I did on top of this.

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:47.960
And yeah, the time and--

00:30:47.960 --> 00:30:49.850
mostly the time.

00:30:49.850 --> 00:30:52.130
I'm not even sure how
much money we are saving,

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:54.980
but the time savings
are a big win.

00:30:54.980 --> 00:30:57.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

